      "eyebrow": "The rules",
      "heading": "The Protocol and the Acts",
      "html": "<p>In England and Wales the lease sets the duties. The Dilapidations Protocol, a pre-action protocol for commercial property in England and Wales published on the Ministry of Justice site, sets how a claim is run before court. Section 18(1) of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1927 caps damages for breach of a repairing covenant at the amount by which the breach reduces the value of the landlord's reversion, and no damages are recoverable for failing to leave premises in repair if they would be demolished or substantially altered so that the repairs would be valueless. That is why a claim is not just the cost of works. Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954 covers the renewal of business tenancies.</p><p>Quickler does not read the lease or advise on a claim. This is general information, not legal advice.</p>"
